    782	| §D2.a.2 NOVELTY_CHALLENGES — PH W-scan over primorials W ∈ {2, 6, 30, 210, 2310}: does the PH serial-correlation deficit z(B2; T0) follow the Hardy-Littlewood twin-prime singular-series (1 - 2/p) per-prime decay rate? (parent S96 → S117 → S124 → S131; refines E2.17) | REFINEMENT | E | Successor in the E2.17 W-trick refinement chain. M=1000 main scan + M=500 finite-size controls at all five W, K=20 baseline replicates per cell, pooled over min(3, φ(W)) coprime residues. **Pre-stated falsifiers**: F1 monotone HL decay; F2 W=210 reproduces S117 (-1.99 ± 1.5σ); F3 W=2 ≥ 50% of S96 (z ≥ 4); F4 z(B1) preserved; F5 closed-form fit r(W) = ∏(1 - α/p) with α ∈ (0, 2]. **Outcome**: F2 ✓ (M=1000 W=210 z = -1.95 vs S117 -1.99); F3 ✓ (W=2 captures 90% of S96 deficit at M=1000, 66% at M=500); F4 ✓ (z(B1; W=2310) ≥ z(B1; W=2)); F1 partial at M=1000 (W=2310 rebound to -3.04) but ✓ at M=500 (clean monotone 4.89 → 1.52 → 0.93 → 0.99 → 0.30); F5 partial — α = 2.07 fits W = 6 → W = 30 cell-pair to 0.001 / 0.008 in r-units, but K = 20 noise floor (~0.045) prevents tight α-constraints at W ≥ 210. **Net new content**: (i) **Per-prime decay rate identified as the Hardy-Littlewood twin-prime local factor (1 - 2/p)**: α ≈ 2 = number of forbidden residues mod p in a coprime pair (HL 1923 §4). PH-side analogue of E2.13's Gowers W-scan with the same per-prime structure. (ii) **Saturation by W = 6**: serial-correlation component reaches K = 20 noise floor by W = 6 (p = 3 filter alone removes ~70% of W=2 deficit); W = 210 sat in saturation regime, not HL-active regime — quantitative correction to S117's narrative. (iii) **W = 2310 M = 1000 rebound diagnosed as finite-size window non-stationarity**: φ(2310) = 480 forces M = 1000 window to span 2.13 log-decades, Cramér normalisation `g/(φ(W) log q_n)` drifts ~15%; M = 500 control eliminates rebound (z(B2; W=2310) = +0.30). **Refines E2.17 inline** with the explicit per-prime decay rate `(1 - 2/p)` matching HL twin-prime constant, refined statement at line 1232–1275 of EDGES.md. **Cross-domain ingredient**: Hardy-Littlewood 1923 twin-prime singular series (already in CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES); the new content is the PH-side identification of the per-prime local factor. **Successors proposed (S138)**: §D2.a.2.i K = 200 follow-up to tighten noise floor and constrain α ∈ {1.5, 2, 2.5} (1 session, 10× CPU); §D2.a.2.ii matched-physical-window protocol holding log q_n drift ≤ 1 across all W (1 session). **Diminishing-returns warning**: S138 is the **fifth refinement of E2.17** (S96 → S117 → S124 → S131 → S138). The K = 20 noise floor caps further refinement to ~1σ; chain is at terminus. Either close formally with K = 200 confirmation OR pivot to D2.b/D2.c (different PH constructions). Cites E2.13, E2.14, E2.15, E2.16, E2.17, E2.19, E2.20. See `experiments/topological/persistent_homology_w_scan/`, `archive/sessions/session138_d2a2_ph_w_scan.md`. | 138 |
    783	| §D27 ATTACK_VECTORS — Newman / Erdős L^∞-flatness of χ_P-coefficient polynomial f_N(z) = Σ_{n≤N} χ_P(n) z^n: does R_N := ‖f_N‖_∞ / √π(N) saturate Erdős/Newman flat-polynomial bound? (cross-domain: Newman 1976 Proc. AMS 51 / Erdős 1957 Mich. Math. J. 4 / Salem-Zygmund / Bourgain Λ(p) — Kahane 1985 CUP §6) | FAIL | I | Frontier wild_swing S138 on the L^∞ endpoint (p=∞) of the Fourier-side characterisation of χ_P, complementary to D10/E2.20 (Mahler = log integral) and D25 (Stein-Tomas L^p, finite p). FFT-based ‖f_N‖_∞ = max_k |F[k]| via M = 16(N+1) oversampled DFT; major-arc decomposition exposes |F(e^{2πi a/q})|/√π(N) =: R(a/q) at every rational. Ensembles: 50 matched-density Bernoulli (uniform support), 50 matched-parity Bernoulli (odd support, single p=2 included), 50 signed-prime Littlewood, deterministic Liouville f_λ, Rudin-Shapiro at length π(N). Run at N ∈ {2^{10}, 2^{12}, 2^{14}, 2^{16}, 2^{18}}. **Pre-registered falsifiers**: (a) |R_N(prime) − R_B| ≤ 0.1 → B-grade Salem-Zygmund typical; (b) R_N(prime) → c < √2 → A-grade super-RudinShapiro flat; (c) R_N(prime) >> R_B → I-mode HL singular-series imprint. **Outcome: B-grade case (c) mode I — HL singular series is recovered EXACTLY at every rational point.** **Empirical identity (verified at all 5 N)**: R(a/q; primes) = √π(N) · μ(q)² / φ(q) + O(√(2 log N)) for every (a, q) = 1 with 1 ≤ q ≤ 64. Squarefree q: relative error 0.1–6% (median 1.5%) across 64 q values at N=2^{18}; non-squarefree q (μ(q)²=0): R ≈ 0.05–0.7 (vanishes within Salem-Zygmund noise). At N=2^{18} (π(N)=23000, √π(N)=151.66): R(q=1)=151.66 (HL=151.66 exact); R(q=2)=151.64 (HL=151.66, 99.99% match — parity peak); R(q=3)={75.60, 75.76} (HL=75.83); R(q=4)=0.36 (μ²=0); R(q=5)={37.71-38.10} (HL=37.91); R(q=8)=0.23 (μ²=0); ... full table in run_full.log covers q ≤ 64. **Bare ‖f_N‖_∞ = (1+o(1)) · √π(N)**, attained at z = -1 across all N, confirmed at both N=2^{16} and 2^{18}. Q_max scan at N=2^{18}: R(prime, minor) = {151.64, 75.76, 75.76, 38.09, 19.17, 12.83, 12.31} for Q_max = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}; R(Bern, minor) ≈ 10.7 ± 0.6 across all Q_max. Z-scores progress {214, 80, 114, 53, 11, 3.1, 3.1}σ — primes converge to Bernoulli noise floor as Q_max → ∞, with persistent ~3σ residual reflecting cumulative O(Q²) HL peaks at q > Q_max. Scaling rule R(prime, minor; Q_max) ≈ √π(N) / φ(q*) where q* = smallest squarefree integer > Q_max verified to ~10% across all (N, Q_max) cells. **Mechanism for closure mode I (partial closure with HL fingerprint as new content)**: the L^∞ structure decomposes EXACTLY into the Hardy-Littlewood circle-method singular series. Major-arc R(a/q) = √π(N)·μ²(q)/φ(q) is the exponential-sum identity Σ_{p≤N} e^{2πi p a/q} ≈ μ(q)/φ(q)·π(N) (classical Vinogradov / Hardy-Littlewood); the bare ‖f‖_∞ is dominated by q=2 parity. **Erdős-extremal flatness DECISIVELY REFUTED**: R_N → ∞ as √π(N), the OPPOSITE extreme; primes are one of the LEAST flat 0/1 supports possible (saturating the trivial upper bound from above via parity). **Distinct from**: D10/E2.20 (Mahler = log integral, geometric mean) — D27 makes the parity major-arc contribution to the Jensen integral explicit at z=-1; D25 (Stein-Tomas L^p, finite p) — D27 covers the p=∞ endpoint; CLOSED_PATHS line 778/E7.16 (Friedman/Ramanujan prime-Cayley spectral gap) — that uses the prime SET as Cayley generators on Z/NZ, this uses χ_P as polynomial coefficients. **Adds EDGE E2.21**: R(a/q; primes) = √π(N)·μ²(q)/φ(q) + O(√log N) at every rational; ‖f_N‖_∞ = (1+o(1))√π(N) attained at z=-1; provides the L^∞-norm endpoint of the L^p Fourier-side category. **Negative-shape consequence**: any L^∞-Chebyshev-style compression of f_N cannot beat √π(N) storage — parity peak forces this lower bound; rules out "flat-polynomial" representation of χ_P as polylog primality witness. **Cross-domain ingredient (Newman / Erdős / Littlewood / Bourgain Λ(p) extremal-polynomial L^∞ harmonic analysis) performed real work**: first project quantitative use; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §2 row "Newman / Erdős polynomial flatness" promoted PROPOSED (D27) → USED I with edge E2.21. **Channelled mathematician**: Vinogradov (major-arc / minor-arc decomposition for prime exponential sums; HL circle method). **Successors proposed (S138)**: D27.a Vinogradov minor-arc supremum at N=2^{18}, 10⁴ random rationals a/q with q ∈ (N^{2/5}, N^{3/5}); does empirical max R(a/q) match `C·N^{1/4}(log N)^A` Vinogradov bound? D27.b Liouville L^∞ at major arcs — Möbius/nilsequence orthogonality predicts R^λ(a/q) = o(1) for ALL q including q=2, screening data already shows ‖f_λ‖_∞/√N = 3.16 with argmax NOT at q=2; quantitative single-session test. D27.c twin-prime Newman polynomial L^∞ uses HL twin-prime singular series ∝ ∏(p-2)/(p-1) — different q-pattern. Cites E1.5, E1.10, E2.13, E2.20. Cross-domain refs: Erdős 1957 *Mich. Math. J.* 4; Newman 1976 *Proc. AMS* 51; Kahane 1985 *Some Random Series of Functions* CUP §6 (Salem-Zygmund); Bourgain 1988 *Acta Math.* 161 (Λ(p) sets); Balister-Bollobás-Morris-Sahasrabudhe 2020 *Annals* 192, 977 (resolution of Erdős for ±1). See experiments/analytic/newman_linfty_chi_p/. | 138 |
    784	| §D30 ATTACK_VECTORS — Pollicott-Ruelle resonances of χ_P-weighted Gauss-map transfer operator: does L_{χ_P} f(x) = Σ_n χ_P(n) f(1/(x+n))/(x+n)² have an isolated arithmetic resonance encoding π(x), à la Mayer 1991 dynamical-determinant representation of ζ via the unweighted Gauss map? (cross-domain: Pollicott 1985 Inventiones 81 / Ruelle 1976 Inventiones 34 / Mayer 1991 Bull. AMS 25 transfer operator spectral theory; Baladi 2018 *Dynamical Zeta Functions* Springer Ergeb. 68) | FAIL | E | Frontier wild_swing S140 on the FIRST dynamical-spectral / transfer-operator measurement on χ_P, recommended next pick per S139 critique single-pick annotation. Chebyshev-Lobatto barycentric collocation on [0,1], (M_grid+1)-dim matrix; cylinder truncation n_max sweeping {100, 200, 400, 800}; 200 controls per ensemble at M=80, n=400; 5 baselines (B_naive=Bernoulli ρ, B_supp=cardinality-matched random subset of [2..n_max], B_par=parity-matched, B_cra=Cramér 1/log n, B_crao=Cramér+odd-parity most stringent); plus Rademacher controls for Liouville. **Pre-registered falsifiers**: F-A unweighted h=1 reproduces Mayer/GKW spectrum within 1e-3 (sanity); F-B B_crao closure within ±2σ (mode E); F-C ≥3σ deviation persisting under refinement (mode I); F-D closed-form polylog identity (A-grade). **Outcome: B-grade case (i) mode E — F-A passes, F-B holds, F-C/F-D fail.** F-A: top-3 unweighted eigenvalues at M=160, n=800 = (0.99820, -0.30293, +0.10064) vs published GKW (1.0000, -0.30366300289, +0.10088637) — agreement < 0.2% on top-3, sanity ✓. **χ_P spectrum (M=120, n=400)**: |λ_0|=0.35961, |λ_1|=0.05103, |λ_2|=0.00792, |λ_3|=0.00128, |λ_4|=0.00021 — alternating signs, geometric decay (gap |λ_1|/|λ_0|=0.142), parallel to GKW structure. **Refinement-stable**: top-5 eigenvalues vary by <1% CV across (M_grid, n_max) ∈ {30..160}×{100..800}, real Pollicott-Ruelle resonances (not spurious-discretisation). **Closed-form prediction**: leading eigenvector of L_h overlaps unweighted Gauss density g(x)=1/((1+x)log2) at cosine ≥0.9919 for h ∈ {χ_P, λ, Λ}; arithmetic content lives in eigenvalue, not eigenfunction. Rayleigh-quotient closed form λ_0^h ≈ Σ_n h(n)·a_n with a_n=T_n/||g||², T_n=∫₀¹dx/[(1+x)(x+n)(x+n+1)·log²2] explicit (partial fractions); for n≥2: T_n=(1/log²2)·[ln2/(n(n-1)) - ln((n+1)/n)/(n-1) + ln((n+2)/(n+1))/n], asymptotic a_n~2log2/n², Σa_n=1. **At n_max=400: Σ_p a_p = 0.36187 vs measured |λ_0|^{χ_P}=0.35961, +0.6% rel error; Σ_n Λ(n)·a_n = 0.5206 vs measured |λ_0|^Λ=0.4968, +4.8% rel error.** For signed λ Rayleigh fails by factor 2 (left eigenvector ≠ const for signed weights). **Cramér closure**: vs B_crao 200 seeds at M=80, n=400: |λ_0| z=-1.79, |λ_1| z=-1.60, gap z=+2.10 — all within ±2σ Bonferroni (3-feature ≈ 2.39σ); F-B holds. vs B_supp z=+3.58 (uniform random over-spreads to high indices, false depression); vs B_par z=+2.02 (parity matched but lacks log-density profile). **A-grade hypothesis falsified**: |λ_0|/(π(N)/N) = 0.3596/0.195 = 1.844 — NOT a closed-form constant; the closed form IS Σ_p a_p, which is cost-equivalent to enumerating primes ≤ N. **Mechanism for closure mode E**: arithmetic-weighted PR leading resonance is a DENSITY-ONLY invariant at first moment — captured exactly by Σ_n h(n)·a_n where {a_n} is independent of h. Cramér model 1/log n + odd parity reproduces this expectation within sample noise. **Mayer-style dynamical-determinant representation of π(x) NOT opened**: there is no isolated arithmetic resonance distinct from the density-Rayleigh prediction. **Connections**: STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT from E2.13 Gowers (S85 detected HL → W-trick erases), E2.14 Anderson Lyapunov (S88 detected HL → W-trick erases), E2.16 anti-DPP (S95), E2.17 PH (S96), E2.19 subword (S104), E2.20 Mahler (S134), E2.21 Newman (S138) — all DETECT HL deviations; E2.22 (this work) shows the dynamical-spectral leading resonance is a 1-summing density integral that the Cramér model captures by construction. STRUCTURALLY DISTINCT from CLOSED line 320, 425