| §D5 ATTACK_VECTORS — Continuous-time quantum walk on the divisor / coprime graph: does U(t) = exp(−iH t) with seed `|1⟩` and target `v_p = (1/√π(x)) Σ_{p≤x} |p⟩` concentrate amplitude polylogarithmically on primes (Childs glued-tree analogue)? (cross-domain: Childs 2009 PRL 102 = arXiv:0806.1972 + Childs-Cleve-Deotto-Farhi-Gutmann-Spielman 2003 STOC = arXiv:quant-ph/0209131) | FAIL | E | Frontier wild_swing S141 on the natural CTQW successor to D4 (S80, Szegedy closed E). H_G ∈ {A, L = D − A} on G ∈ {D_x, C_x}. Eigendecomposition U(t) = Σ_k e^{−iλ_k t} u_k u_k^T; amplitude `P(t) = |⟨v_p|U(t)|1⟩|²` swept on t ∈ [0, 500] at 5001 grid points. **Pre-registered falsifiers**: F-A classical equilibration baseline `π(x)/x` (sanity); F-B peak amplitude beats Cramér + odd-parity matched control by a factor that grows with x (A-grade); F-C isolated band-edge eigenvector with O(1) overlap with v_p (glued-tree A-grade signature); F-D closure mode confirmed across both Hamiltonian variants. **Outcome**: F-A passes, F-B fails (excess +4–5σ but BOUNDED and not scaling), F-C fails (top overlap decays as `x^{−0.20}`, no isolated cluster), F-D confirms across all 4 variants. **Scaling (divisor + H=A, x ∈ {32, 64, 96, 128, 192, 256, 384, 512, 768}, T=500)**: ratio_max(x) := max_t P(t) / (π(x)/x) ∈ {1.24, 1.34, 1.39, 1.30, 1.23, 1.29, 1.31, 1.13, 1.22}; OLS fit `1.151 + 0.609/log(x)` → x→∞ asymptote ≈ **1.151** (bounded constant). top_overlap(x) := max_k |⟨u_k|v_p⟩| ∈ {0.626, 0.532, 0.469, 0.455, 0.420, 0.393, 0.369, 0.352, 0.328}; OLS log-log fit `1.214 · x^{−0.200}` (slow polynomial decay, NOT constant). **C2 control (Cramér + odd parity, 100 seeds)**: z_peak = +5.4, +3.3, +5.1 at x = 64, 128, 256; z_mean = +4.6, +4.1, +4.0 — modest persistent excess (~+4σ residual) but does NOT scale. C1 (uniform random subset) z = +2.9, +3.9, +7.5, +10.2 grows but smaller than C3. C3 (random ER graph, same mean degree) z = +10.6, +22.1, +39.9, +65.8 — divisor graph IS structured, but structure is positional (vertex-1 hub), not prime-detection. **Hamiltonian / graph variants (F-D)**: divisor + H=L (Laplacian): ratio_max → 0 as x grows (zero-mode of L kills coupling, |1⟩ has nontrivial overlap with all-ones eigenvector orthogonal to centred v_p); coprime + H=A: ratio_max DECREASES as `x^{−1/2}` (primes anti-concentrated relative to equilibrium); coprime + H=L: ratio_max → 0. All 4 variants close mode E with no polylog opening. **Eigenvector inspection (x=128, divisor H=A, top-overlap k=1, λ_1=−6.03, overlap 0.455)**: dominant entries vertex 1 (−0.436), vertex 4 (+0.293), vertex 2 (+0.284), vertex 6 (+0.264), vertex 3 (+0.181) — "anti-hub vs first-shell" mode; mean |amp| ratio prime/composite = 1.42; the apparent overlap with v_p is positional artefact (primes 2, 3 are in the first shell of vertex 1) NOT primality detection. **Mechanism for closure mode E**: time-averaged amplitude `⟨P(t)⟩_t = Σ_k |⟨u_k|v_p⟩|² · |⟨u_k|1⟩|²` is dispersed across O(N) modes; no isolated band-edge cluster has constant overlap with v_p; peak amplitude over t is bounded by ~`max_k |⟨u_k|v_p⟩|² · |⟨u_k|1⟩|²` × (constructive interference factor O(1)), giving `max_t P(t) ≤ C · π(x)/x` with C ≈ 1.15 across x ∈ {32..768}. **Closure structurally distinct from D4 (Szegedy)**: D4 closed via discriminant-matrix spectral gap `δ = 1/poly(x)` blocking polylog mixing; D5 closes via eigenstate overlap dispersal, which is the *Childs spectral-density* closure language. The two arguments are independent — Szegedy could fail on gap while CTQW succeeds on band-edge cluster (as for glued trees), but here BOTH fail on the divisor / coprime graph families because the relevant spectral structure is dispersed. **Adds EDGE E7.20 (CTQW amplitude ceiling)**: for G ∈ {D_x, C_x}, `H ∈ {A_G, L_G}`, seed `|1⟩`: `max_t |⟨v_p|exp(−iHt)|1⟩|² ≤ C·π(x)/x` (C ≈ 1.151 asymptote on D_x H=A; → 0 on Laplacian / coprime H=A variants). Top eigenvector overlap with v_p decays as `x^{−0.20}`. EVS shape (forbids polylog primality extraction by CTQW on these graphs). **Distinct from**: CLOSED 105 (constructive transfer matrix), 182 (FRACTRAN), 474 (Goldreich-Levin Hadamard), and from E7.13 (Szegedy on same graphs, different mechanism). **Connection to E7.16 / E2.22**: time-averaged amplitude residual vs Cramér+odd-parity matched control is +4σ, same magnitude as E2.22's PR-resonance Cramér closure — primes are density-and-parity Cramér-typical at the time-averaged CTQW amplitude level. **Cross-domain ingredient (Childs continuous-time quantum walks) performed real work**: first project use of CTQW spectral-density framework (D4 Szegedy uses discriminant-matrix algebra, fundamentally different); CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §1 row "Continuous-time quantum walks (CTQW)" promoted PROPOSED (D5) → USED E with edge E7.20. **Channelled mathematician**: Childs (CTQW spectral-density framework). **Successors proposed (S141)**: D5.a CTQW on LPS Ramanujan Cayley graph `Cay(PSL_2(F_p), prime-indexed quaternion generators)` — non-abelian Cayley with explicit Ramanujan spectral gap; tests whether non-abelian Cayley admits isolated band-edge clusters that abelian divisor / coprime do not (composes with D28 PROPOSED, 1-2 sessions); D5.b multi-vertex seed `|v_s⟩ = (1/√k) Σ_{i ∈ S} |i⟩` for arithmetic-relevant S (powers of 2, smooth numbers, square-free integers) — does the right seed unlock polylog amplitude on primes? (1-session screening). Cites E7.13 (D4 Szegedy structural template), E7.16 (Friedman density+parity matching), E2.22 (PR resonance Cramér closure structurally analogous). Cross-domain refs: Childs 2009 *PRL* 102, 180501 = arXiv:0806.1972; Childs-Cleve-Deotto-Farhi-Gutmann-Spielman 2003 STOC = arXiv:quant-ph/0209131. See `experiments/quantum/ctqw_chi_p/` and `archive/sessions/session141_d5_ctqw_chi_p.md`. | 141 |
